  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\n\nHeading into early next week, there is good confidence that a \nPacific low will move to the central/southern coast of California \nby the end of the day Monday. Exactly how this low evolves once\nreaching the coast is still a little uncertain, but latest\nmodel consensus suggests the low will track ENE inland staying \nmostly north of the local area and driving a chance for showers\nand storms across northern AZ. Conceptually this is not a bad\nsetup for a transitional severe weather event, with potential\nfrontogenetical forcing and a moderately strong upper level jet\nwith higher shear, but rebounding quickly from the very dry air \nthis weekend and getting sufficient moisture in here to support \nany lower desert rain chance will be tough. Model guidance does at\nleast support a slow return of moisture Monday through next week,\nmore so coming from the southeast like typical early stages of \nthe monsoon. Model guidance is also pointing toward a strong ridge\ndeveloping through the middle of the U.S. with the subtropical \nhigh restrengthening near/over Texas by mid to late week. This \nwould keep the Desert Southwest under predominant southwest flow \nwhich is not the most ideal setup for lower desert storm chances, \nespecially not for southeast CA and southwest AZ. Latest NBM \nforecast has PoPs focused primarily across northern and eastern AZ\neach day next week with only minor (10-20%) PoPs for south- \ncentral AZ lower deserts in the evenings. The one thing this \npattern does open the door for, the longer it persists, is \npotential Eastern Pacific tropical moisture advecting north.\n\nTemperatures through next week will mostly stay the same day-to-\nday, with highs in the upper 90s to lower 100s, which will be around\n2-5 degrees above normal, and lows remaining mostly in the 70s.\nThe temperatures will lead to widespread Minor HeatRisk through\nat least the first half of the week with a trend toward areas of  \nModerate HeatRisk by the second half of the week as morning low\ntemperatures creep back up.",
